1
00:00:00,182 --> 00:00:02,733
Wrong answers on the SAT aren't random.

2
00:00:02,753 --> 00:00:07,412
They're written to be tempting, and the
same patterns show up again and again.

3
00:00:07,432 --> 00:00:11,619
Once you can name the pattern, you
can spot the trap before you pick it.

4
00:00:11,639 --> 00:00:15,298
This video covers fourteen of
them: seven in Reading and Writing,

5
00:00:15,318 --> 00:00:20,512
and seven in Math, each with a
short original SAT-style example.

6
00:00:20,532 --> 00:00:22,602
Try each one before I explain it.

7
00:00:22,675 --> 00:00:25,776
If you fall for a trap, now you
know which one to watch for.

8
00:00:25,796 --> 00:00:28,062
Part one: Reading and Writing.

9
00:00:28,082 --> 00:00:30,653
Trap one: true but irrelevant.

10
00:00:30,689 --> 00:00:32,026
The choice is accurate.

11
00:00:32,046 --> 00:00:34,046
It just doesn't answer the
question that was asked.

12
00:00:34,492 --> 00:00:35,813
Here's a notes question.

13
00:00:35,849 --> 00:00:38,936
The notes cover the Mariana
Trench, its deepest point,

14
00:00:38,956 --> 00:00:41,793
called the Challenger Deep,
the height of Mount Everest,

15
00:00:41,813 --> 00:00:44,170
and the first people to reach the bottom.

16
00:00:44,242 --> 00:00:48,292
The goal: compare the depth of the
Challenger Deep with the height of Mount

17
00:00:48,312 --> 00:00:49,122
Everest.

18
00:00:49,142 --> 00:00:50,392
Pause and try it.

19
00:00:54,591 --> 00:00:57,070
A, B, and C are all true.

20
00:00:57,090 --> 00:01:02,070
But only D compares the two numbers:
about ten thousand nine hundred meters

21
00:01:02,090 --> 00:01:06,698
down, against about eight thousand
eight hundred fifty meters up.

22
00:01:06,841 --> 00:01:08,769
The answer is D.

23
00:01:08,810 --> 00:01:12,703
How to beat it: restate the goal
before you read the choices.

24
00:01:12,739 --> 00:01:16,212
Then ask: is it true,
and does it do that job?

25
00:01:16,232 --> 00:01:18,462
Trap two: too extreme.

26
00:01:18,482 --> 00:01:21,390
The choice pushes the
passage further than it goes.

27
00:01:21,410 --> 00:01:23,104
A link becomes proof.

28
00:01:23,124 --> 00:01:24,839
Often becomes always.

29
00:01:24,910 --> 00:01:25,910
Try this.

30
00:01:26,588 --> 00:01:31,426
A teacher surveyed students at one high
school and found that students who read

31
00:01:31,446 --> 00:01:35,854
for pleasure at least once a week tended
to have higher English grades than

32
00:01:35,874 --> 00:01:38,517
students who rarely read for pleasure.

33
00:01:38,578 --> 00:01:42,808
The survey, however, did not show
whether the reading habit came before the

34
00:01:42,828 --> 00:01:45,058
higher grades or the other way around.

35
00:01:45,078 --> 00:01:47,894
The survey's results therefore blank

36
00:01:47,914 --> 00:01:49,164
Pause and try it.

37
00:01:52,362 --> 00:01:55,557
A says the results prove that
reading causes better grades.

38
00:01:55,577 --> 00:01:58,592
But the survey can't even
show which came first.

39
00:01:58,612 --> 00:02:00,591
Prove is far too strong.

40
00:02:00,611 --> 00:02:04,449
C, a link but not proof of cause,
matches the passage exactly.

41
00:02:04,469 --> 00:02:05,577
The answer is C.

42
00:02:05,597 --> 00:02:08,969
How to beat it: circle
strong words like proves,

43
00:02:08,989 --> 00:02:11,347
always, never, and only.

44
00:02:11,419 --> 00:02:14,091
Then check whether the
passage is that strong.

45
00:02:14,111 --> 00:02:16,360
Trap three: half right.

46
00:02:16,397 --> 00:02:20,619
The choice starts out perfect, then adds
something the passage doesn't support.

47
00:02:20,639 --> 00:02:22,174
Here's a main idea question.

48
00:02:22,388 --> 00:02:26,440
The name Silk Road suggests a single
road running from China to the

49
00:02:26,460 --> 00:02:31,066
Mediterranean Sea, traveled from end
to end by merchants carrying silk.

50
00:02:31,246 --> 00:02:33,853
Historians today describe
something different:

51
00:02:33,960 --> 00:02:37,924
a vast, shifting network
of land and sea routes.

52
00:02:38,023 --> 00:02:41,967
Along this network, most goods
traveled only short distances,

53
00:02:41,987 --> 00:02:45,860
passing from one merchant to the next
many times before reaching their final

54
00:02:45,880 --> 00:02:47,124
destinations.

55
00:02:47,144 --> 00:02:48,394
Pause and try it.

56
00:02:52,593 --> 00:02:58,001
A and C start the same way: historians
now describe the Silk Road as a network

57
00:02:58,021 --> 00:02:59,037
of routes.

58
00:02:59,057 --> 00:03:03,073
Then C adds that silk was the
most valuable good traded.

59
00:03:03,093 --> 00:03:04,716
The passage never says that.

60
00:03:04,736 --> 00:03:07,501
A finishes with what the passage does say.

61
00:03:07,521 --> 00:03:09,078
The answer is A.

62
00:03:09,098 --> 00:03:12,720
B describes the old view, the
one the passage moves away from.

63
00:03:12,740 --> 00:03:17,219
How to beat half right: read every
choice all the way to the period.

64
00:03:17,239 --> 00:03:19,112
Trap four: the opposite.

65
00:03:19,132 --> 00:03:21,041
The choice flips the passage's meaning,

66
00:03:21,061 --> 00:03:24,954
often by matching what someone
in the passage expected.

67
00:03:25,021 --> 00:03:26,021
Try it.

68
00:03:26,235 --> 00:03:30,286
Engineers expected the new bridge coating
to crack after a winter of freezing

69
00:03:30,306 --> 00:03:31,413
temperatures.

70
00:03:31,485 --> 00:03:37,563
Instead, the coating proved remarkably
blank after months of ice and snow,

71
00:03:37,583 --> 00:03:40,190
inspectors found no damage at all.

72
00:03:40,228 --> 00:03:41,478
Pause and try it.

73
00:03:44,677 --> 00:03:47,156
Fragile matches the
engineers' expectation.

74
00:03:47,176 --> 00:03:48,300
That's the trap.

75
00:03:48,320 --> 00:03:50,871
But instead tells you the
expectation was wrong,

76
00:03:50,891 --> 00:03:53,585
and no damage at all tells
you the coating held up.

77
00:03:53,605 --> 00:03:55,783
The answer is B, durable.

78
00:03:55,819 --> 00:04:00,013
How to beat it: watch for
turn words like instead,

79
00:04:00,033 --> 00:04:02,335
however, although, and despite.

80
00:04:02,355 --> 00:04:05,819
When the passage turns, your
answer has to turn with it.

81
00:04:05,885 --> 00:04:07,921
Trap five: out of scope.

82
00:04:08,242 --> 00:04:11,258
The choice brings in something
the passage never covers.

83
00:04:11,278 --> 00:04:13,921
Sometimes it's even true in real life.

84
00:04:13,987 --> 00:04:15,166
Here's the passage.

85
00:04:15,736 --> 00:04:18,646
Many songbirds, such as
warblers and thrushes,

86
00:04:18,666 --> 00:04:20,039
migrate at night.

87
00:04:20,059 --> 00:04:24,094
Flying after dark offers these
birds several advantages.

88
00:04:24,130 --> 00:04:27,859
The night air is usually cooler
and denser than daytime air,

89
00:04:27,879 --> 00:04:29,676
which can make flying easier.

90
00:04:29,696 --> 00:04:34,176
And birds that travel at night can spend
the daylight hours resting and feeding

91
00:04:34,196 --> 00:04:36,625
to replace the energy their flights use.

92
00:04:37,052 --> 00:04:40,767
According to the text, what is one
advantage of migrating at night?

93
00:04:40,815 --> 00:04:42,065
Pause and try it.

94
00:04:45,264 --> 00:04:48,530
B says birds use the
stars to find their way.

95
00:04:48,550 --> 00:04:51,264
Night-migrating songbirds really do.

96
00:04:51,371 --> 00:04:53,994
But this passage never mentions stars.

97
00:04:54,014 --> 00:04:57,442
The answer is A, because
the text says it directly.

98
00:04:57,466 --> 00:05:01,894
How to beat it: for every choice,
point to the sentence that supports it.

99
00:05:02,109 --> 00:05:05,537
No sentence, no answer, no
matter how true it sounds.

100
00:05:05,615 --> 00:05:08,257
Trap six: sounds right.

101
00:05:08,436 --> 00:05:12,130
On grammar questions, the wrong
answer often reads smoothly out loud,

102
00:05:12,150 --> 00:05:13,971
so your ear says yes.

103
00:05:14,007 --> 00:05:17,365
But the test is about rules, not sound.

104
00:05:17,411 --> 00:05:18,249
Try this.

105
00:05:18,269 --> 00:05:23,409
The town's first public library opened
in a single room above a blank now

106
00:05:23,429 --> 00:05:26,196
occupies an entire city block.

107
00:05:26,216 --> 00:05:27,466
Pause and try it.

108
00:05:30,664 --> 00:05:34,466
Choice A, bakery comma it, sounds fine.

109
00:05:34,486 --> 00:05:36,359
But both sides are complete sentences,

110
00:05:36,379 --> 00:05:38,323
and a comma alone can't join them.

111
00:05:38,343 --> 00:05:39,752
That's a comma splice.

112
00:05:39,772 --> 00:05:41,680
A semicolon can join them.

113
00:05:41,700 --> 00:05:43,021
The answer is B.

114
00:05:43,063 --> 00:05:45,150
How to beat it: don't ask
whether it sounds right.

115
00:05:45,170 --> 00:05:46,507
Name the rule it follows.

116
00:05:46,527 --> 00:05:48,484
If you can't, slow down.

117
00:05:48,504 --> 00:05:51,769
Trap seven: two choices
that do the same job.

118
00:05:51,789 --> 00:05:54,019
If two choices truly mean the same thing,

119
00:05:54,039 --> 00:05:55,911
they can't both be the one right answer.

120
00:05:55,931 --> 00:05:57,289
So neither one is.

121
00:05:57,383 --> 00:05:58,954
Here's a transition question.

122
00:05:59,132 --> 00:06:04,363
Most plants open tiny pores called
stomata during the day to take in carbon

123
00:06:04,383 --> 00:06:07,383
dioxide, losing water in the process.

124
00:06:07,544 --> 00:06:12,604
blank many cacti keep their stomata
closed during the hot day and open them at

125
00:06:12,624 --> 00:06:15,787
night, when cooler air reduces water loss.

126
00:06:15,807 --> 00:06:17,057
Pause and try it.

127
00:06:20,255 --> 00:06:24,004
Therefore and as a result
both signal a consequence.

128
00:06:24,041 --> 00:06:25,914
Same job, so both are out.

129
00:06:25,934 --> 00:06:28,699
Cacti do the opposite of most plants:

130
00:06:28,719 --> 00:06:30,092
night instead of day.

131
00:06:30,112 --> 00:06:32,397
So the answer is D, by contrast.

132
00:06:32,448 --> 00:06:35,856
How to beat it: spot the
twins, cross out both,

133
00:06:35,876 --> 00:06:37,769
and four choices become two.

134
00:06:37,798 --> 00:06:39,171
Part two: Math.

135
00:06:39,191 --> 00:06:41,778
Trap eight: answering the wrong quantity.

136
00:06:41,798 --> 00:06:44,798
You solve correctly,
but for the wrong thing.

137
00:06:44,846 --> 00:06:45,648
Try it.

138
00:06:45,668 --> 00:06:50,790
If two x plus y equals eleven,
and x minus y equals one,

139
00:06:50,810 --> 00:06:53,516
what is the value of x plus y?

140
00:06:53,536 --> 00:06:54,786
Pause and try it.

141
00:06:57,984 --> 00:07:00,927
Add the equations: three x equals twelve,

142
00:07:00,947 --> 00:07:03,643
so x is four, and y is three.

143
00:07:03,663 --> 00:07:05,606
Four is right there in the choices.

144
00:07:05,626 --> 00:07:08,393
But they asked for x
plus y, which is seven.

145
00:07:08,413 --> 00:07:09,932
The answer is C.

146
00:07:09,952 --> 00:07:14,361
How to beat it: underline what the
question asks for before you solve,

147
00:07:14,381 --> 00:07:16,381
and reread it before you click.

148
00:07:16,435 --> 00:07:18,827
Trap nine: stopping halfway.

149
00:07:18,934 --> 00:07:22,736
On multi-step questions, the wrong answers
are often numbers from the middle of

150
00:07:22,756 --> 00:07:23,503
the work.

151
00:07:23,523 --> 00:07:29,059
The function f is defined by f
of x equals three x minus five.

152
00:07:29,131 --> 00:07:35,523
If f of a equals sixteen, what is the
value of f of the quantity a plus two?

153
00:07:35,564 --> 00:07:36,814
Pause and try it.

154
00:07:41,012 --> 00:07:44,706
Three a minus five equals
sixteen, so a is seven.

155
00:07:44,726 --> 00:07:48,885
Then a plus two is nine,
and f of nine is twenty-two.

156
00:07:48,905 --> 00:07:52,349
Seven, nine, and sixteen
all show up in the work.

157
00:07:52,369 --> 00:07:54,778
Only twenty-two answers the question.

158
00:07:54,798 --> 00:07:56,334
The answer is D.

159
00:07:56,357 --> 00:07:59,122
How to beat it: every
time you get a number,

160
00:07:59,142 --> 00:08:02,087
ask yourself: is this the final answer,

161
00:08:02,107 --> 00:08:03,447
or just a step?

162
00:08:03,467 --> 00:08:05,503
Trap ten: sign errors.

163
00:08:05,753 --> 00:08:09,483
A dropped negative turns an
easy question into a miss,

164
00:08:09,503 --> 00:08:12,693
and the wrong answer is
often waiting in the choices.

165
00:08:12,713 --> 00:08:13,713
Solve this one.

166
00:08:13,820 --> 00:08:18,050
Five minus two times the
quantity x minus three,

167
00:08:18,070 --> 00:08:19,641
equals one.

168
00:08:19,666 --> 00:08:20,916
Pause and try it.

169
00:08:24,114 --> 00:08:26,257
Distribute the negative two to both terms.

170
00:08:26,328 --> 00:08:29,166
Negative two times negative
three is positive six.

171
00:08:29,186 --> 00:08:31,736
So eleven minus two x equals one.

172
00:08:31,756 --> 00:08:34,451
Two x equals ten, and x equals five.

173
00:08:34,471 --> 00:08:35,990
The answer is D.

174
00:08:36,010 --> 00:08:38,240
Writing minus six gives you negative one.

175
00:08:38,260 --> 00:08:41,347
Losing a sign when you divide
gives you negative five.

176
00:08:41,367 --> 00:08:43,775
How to beat it: plug your answer back in.

177
00:08:43,795 --> 00:08:46,310
Five minus two times two is one.

178
00:08:46,330 --> 00:08:47,330
It checks.

179
00:08:47,374 --> 00:08:50,374
Trap eleven: percent and unit traps.

180
00:08:50,445 --> 00:08:54,088
Percent change is always
measured from the original value.

181
00:08:54,170 --> 00:08:57,542
A ticket's price went from
forty dollars to fifty dollars.

182
00:08:57,562 --> 00:08:59,992
By what percent did the price increase?

183
00:09:00,025 --> 00:09:01,275
Pause and try it.

184
00:09:04,473 --> 00:09:07,381
The change is ten dollars,
and the original is forty.

185
00:09:07,401 --> 00:09:10,060
Ten over forty is twenty-five percent.

186
00:09:10,080 --> 00:09:11,580
The answer is C.

187
00:09:11,615 --> 00:09:14,473
Twenty percent divides
by the new price instead.

188
00:09:14,508 --> 00:09:18,739
And one hundred twenty-five percent is
the new price as a percent of the old

189
00:09:18,759 --> 00:09:19,326
one.

190
00:09:19,346 --> 00:09:20,791
Units trap you the same way.

191
00:09:20,811 --> 00:09:24,148
Forty-five miles per hour for twenty
minutes isn't nine hundred miles.

192
00:09:24,168 --> 00:09:25,648
Twenty minutes is a third of an hour,

193
00:09:25,668 --> 00:09:27,076
so it's fifteen miles.

194
00:09:27,096 --> 00:09:30,418
How to beat it: write the
units next to every number.

195
00:09:30,446 --> 00:09:32,568
Trap twelve: extraneous solutions.

196
00:09:32,588 --> 00:09:37,105
Squaring both sides or clearing a
denominator can create solutions that don't

197
00:09:37,125 --> 00:09:39,052
work in the original equation.

198
00:09:39,072 --> 00:09:43,302
What is the solution set of the equation
x squared over the quantity x minus

199
00:09:43,322 --> 00:09:47,633
three, equals nine over
the quantity x minus three?

200
00:09:47,653 --> 00:09:48,903
Pause and try it.

201
00:09:53,102 --> 00:09:55,617
Multiply both sides by x minus three,

202
00:09:55,637 --> 00:09:58,368
and you get x squared equals nine.

203
00:09:58,388 --> 00:10:01,582
So x is three or negative three.

204
00:10:01,602 --> 00:10:03,725
But three makes the denominator zero,

205
00:10:03,745 --> 00:10:04,582
so it doesn't count.

206
00:10:04,602 --> 00:10:07,010
The only solution is negative three.

207
00:10:07,030 --> 00:10:08,495
The answer is A.

208
00:10:08,534 --> 00:10:12,013
C, both numbers with
no check, is the trap.

209
00:10:12,033 --> 00:10:16,677
How to beat it: when there's a variable
in a denominator or under a square root,

210
00:10:16,749 --> 00:10:19,492
plug every solution
back into the original.

211
00:10:19,512 --> 00:10:21,491
Trap thirteen: r versus r squared.

212
00:10:21,511 --> 00:10:26,634
In a circle equation, the number on
the right side is the radius squared,

213
00:10:26,654 --> 00:10:27,833
not the radius.

214
00:10:27,861 --> 00:10:31,412
The equation, x plus one,
squared, plus y minus four,

215
00:10:31,432 --> 00:10:35,627
squared, equals forty-nine,
represents a circle in the x y plane.

216
00:10:35,647 --> 00:10:37,861
What is the diameter of the circle?

217
00:10:37,883 --> 00:10:39,133
Pause and try it.

218
00:10:42,332 --> 00:10:44,811
Forty-nine is r squared,
so the radius is seven,

219
00:10:44,831 --> 00:10:46,276
and the diameter is fourteen.

220
00:10:46,296 --> 00:10:46,740
The answer is B.

221
00:10:46,760 --> 00:10:51,668
Forty-nine is the r squared trap.

222
00:10:51,688 --> 00:10:53,866
Seven is just the radius.

223
00:10:53,899 --> 00:10:56,379
And if they ask for the
center, it's negative one,

224
00:10:56,399 --> 00:10:57,022
four.

225
00:10:57,042 --> 00:10:58,737
Not one, negative four.

226
00:10:58,757 --> 00:11:00,737
How to beat it: say it every time.

227
00:11:00,757 --> 00:11:02,507
Right side, r squared.

228
00:11:02,721 --> 00:11:04,649
Signs in the parentheses, flipped.

229
00:11:04,741 --> 00:11:08,347
Trap fourteen: reading
the wrong row or column.

230
00:11:08,455 --> 00:11:11,042
In a two-way table, the
question names a group,

231
00:11:11,062 --> 00:11:13,374
and the trap answer uses a different one.

232
00:11:13,394 --> 00:11:14,552
Here's the table.

233
00:11:14,572 --> 00:11:19,215
Of thirty juniors, eighteen chose
robotics and twelve chose debate.

234
00:11:19,251 --> 00:11:23,536
Of thirty seniors, six chose robotics
and twenty-four chose debate.

235
00:11:23,608 --> 00:11:27,337
If a student who chose
robotics is selected at random,

236
00:11:27,357 --> 00:11:30,179
what is the probability that
the student is a junior?

237
00:11:30,231 --> 00:11:31,481
Pause and try it.

238
00:11:35,679 --> 00:11:38,087
The group is students who chose robotics.

239
00:11:38,107 --> 00:11:41,516
There are twenty-four of them,
and eighteen are juniors.

240
00:11:41,536 --> 00:11:43,765
Eighteen over twenty-four
is three fourths.

241
00:11:43,785 --> 00:11:45,715
The answer is D.

242
00:11:45,784 --> 00:11:46,942
Three fifths is the trap.

243
00:11:46,962 --> 00:11:49,478
That's eighteen out of
thirty, the juniors row,

244
00:11:49,498 --> 00:11:51,570
which answers a different question.

245
00:11:51,605 --> 00:11:55,371
How to beat it: the group named in the
question gives you the bottom of the

246
00:11:55,391 --> 00:11:56,212
fraction.

247
00:11:56,232 --> 00:11:57,482
That's all fourteen.

248
00:11:57,589 --> 00:12:00,783
On test day, in Reading and Writing,
when you're down to two choices,

249
00:12:00,803 --> 00:12:02,926
name the trap in the one
you're about to cross out.

250
00:12:02,946 --> 00:12:06,150
If you can't name it, reread
the passage before you decide.

251
00:12:06,170 --> 00:12:08,649
In Math, take ten
seconds before you click.

252
00:12:08,669 --> 00:12:10,007
Did I answer what they asked?

253
00:12:10,027 --> 00:12:12,563
Is this a final answer or just a step?

254
00:12:12,598 --> 00:12:15,400
Did I check signs, units, and solutions?

255
00:12:15,420 --> 00:12:16,953
Did I read the right row?

256
00:12:16,973 --> 00:12:21,453
And on practice tests, write the trap's
name next to every question you miss.

257
00:12:21,473 --> 00:12:25,509
After a few tests, you'll know which
traps cost you the most points.

258
00:12:25,566 --> 00:12:27,581
All fourteen traps on one screen.

259
00:12:27,601 --> 00:12:29,438
Pause here if you want a screenshot.

260
